THE MECHANISM. INV-foluz (a33de088bc) made py.py emit a call edge for EVERY
bare builtin call with `builtins` in the module slot -- len, str,
isinstance, getattr, thousands of sites per repo. Before it only `open`
reached the coverage gate, and `open` carries a row. `builtins` had three
rows (open x2, input) and no entry in python.yaml's module_completeness, so
_uncatalogued_external_modules returned it, _call_production_coverage set
qualifying_only=False, and every claim took the verify_claims.py:3898
inconclusive-without-caveats arm. Reproduced locally with the gate's own
command: the identical 40-line drift. Replayed on hypergumbo's own map with
the shipped predicates, before and after this change: unknown modules 69 before, 68 after, the single difference being builtins (20,325 builtins call edges among 105,931 Python call edges); nothing added.

THE GATE IS RIGHT AND IS NOT TOUCHED. An unenumerated module IS unexamined.
The fix is the positive, dated declaration the catalogue's own rule
requires: every I/O surface of `builtins` rowed or absent. The survey probe
cannot see a C module, so dir(builtins) on 3.12 was walked by hand (144
public callables):

  print, breakpoint, copyright, credits  -> logging      (new rows)
  license                                -> fs_read + logging, simultaneous
  help                                   -> logging + subprocess, `unruled`
                                            (pydoc.pipepager is
                                            Popen(shell=True) on a tty)
  open, input                            -> already rowed
  exit, quit                             -> raise SystemExit; no I/O
  exec, eval, compile                    -> in-process evaluation
  66 exception classes, the constructors -> no I/O

`__import__` is a dunder, outside the public-callable rule the whole audit
applies, and is the import statement's callable form; it is named in the
entry rather than left implicit, with importlib's refusal as the related
open question. `builtins.help` joins HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES: a launch is a
launch. Survey addendum records the walk.

TESTS ARE AGAINST THE SHIPPED CATALOGUE, NOT A STAND-IN. A stand-in could
not have caught this -- it was python.yaml's own completeness list that
lacked the entry -- so the coverage test loads python.yaml and asks the
gate the question the CLI asks on every Python repo, with an unenumerated
control (telnetlib) beside it to show the gate is not loosened.

